course: reflection
lesson: 1
title: When someone dies: guilt, numbness and waves of grief
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Grief is the deep sadness after someone you love dies, and it does not move in a straight line: it comes in waves, out of nowhere, in the bath or on the bus, then goes quiet for a while, then comes back.
explain: Jamie skipped visiting Grandad on Sunday to play football, and Grandad died on the Tuesday; every night in bed the same thought plays like a song stuck on repeat: "I could've gone. I picked stupid football. He probably asked where I was."
explain: That loop is guilt, and it is very common after a death: the mind hunts for one thing it could have changed, because "if only I had" feels less helpless than "nothing could be done".
explain: But nobody knew that Sunday was the last chance, and one missed visit does not cancel years of love; over weeks the sharp pain usually turns into a dull ache, and the day he forgets for a whole afternoon is not betrayal, it is healing.
explain: What helped Jamie was telling Mum the Sunday thought out loud, and she told him Grandad watched his match videos on her phone and was proud; what did not help was pretending at school that he was fine.
fact: Grief is the deep sadness after someone dies; "passed away" is a softer way of saying "died".
fact: Grief often comes in waves: sudden crying at odd moments, then calmer times; over weeks and months the waves usually come less often and less hard.
fact: Guilt after a death is very common; the mind looks for something it could have changed, because that feels less helpless than accepting nobody could.
fact: An accident is a mistake with a terrible result, not something done on purpose; "beating yourself up" means blaming yourself again and again.
fact: Some people feel numb at first, as if their feelings were switched off, because the mind protects itself from too much at once; the tears can come weeks later.
fact: Not crying at a funeral does not mean you did not love the person; everyone grieves differently.
fact: People often avoid someone who is grieving because they are scared of saying the wrong thing; a short "I'm so sorry, I don't know what to say, but I'm here" is enough.
fact: When nobody mentions a death, the grieving person can feel invisible, even though most people do care; many grieving people want others to say the dead person's name.
fact: How sad you feel about a loss depends on how much you cared, not on how big the animal was; laughing at someone's sadness makes them hide it.
fact: Dementia is an illness that damages memory and thinking; a person may forget names but often still responds to a loved voice, a hug or an old song.
fact: The last moment with someone can feel huge because it can no longer be fixed, but one rude or careless moment does not erase a whole year.
fact: Knowing that someone will die soon can bring grief early; "terminally ill" means ill and not going to get better, and fearing that other family members will die too is common and does not make it happen.
fact: When a vet "puts an animal to sleep" (also "puts it down"), the vet helps a very ill animal die peacefully without pain; families choose it to end suffering.
fact: Things that often help are saying the looping thought to a trusted adult, writing a letter to the person, keeping something of theirs and saying their name; pretending and avoiding usually make it heavier.
exercise: Jamie missed his last chance to visit Grandad because of football. Why does the "I could've gone" thought keep coming back at night? | The mind hunts for something it could have changed | It is proof that Jamie really did cause Grandad's death | Grandad must have been angry with Jamie for not coming | Boys who loved their grandad never have thoughts like that
exercise: What is true about Jamie's missed Sunday visit? | Nobody knew it was the last chance, and love lasted years | It means Jamie never really loved his grandad at all, deep down | It is the reason Grandad got ill in the first place | It cannot be talked about, so it is best kept secret
exercise: Two months after Grandad died, Jamie forgot about him for a whole day, then felt bad. What is going on? | It is normal healing; the pain is getting less sharp, not his love | He has stopped caring, which means the grief was fake | He should force himself to think about Grandad every hour | Forgetting for one day means he will soon forget Grandad completely
exercise: Mia left the gate open and her dog Rolo was hit by a car. What is the fairest way to see it? | It was an accident: a mistake with a terrible result, not done on purpose | She killed him on purpose, so she deserves to feel awful | It was nobody's mistake at all, so there is nothing to learn | It was Rolo's fault for running out into the road
exercise: A month later Mia checks the gate three times every time she passes it. Why? | Her worry is trying to stop it ever happening again | She has forgotten how gates work since the accident | Her dad has told her she will be punished otherwise | Checking three times will bring a lost pet back home
exercise: At his nan's funeral everyone cried but Theo felt nothing, "like glass". What is the most likely reason? | Numbness: the mind protecting itself from too much at once | He did not love his nan, so there was nothing there to feel | He is a cold person and always will be from now on | Crying only starts at funerals once you turn eighteen
exercise: Three weeks later Theo found Nan's old cardigan and cried for an hour. What does this show? | Grief can come late, as a delayed reaction | He was only pretending to be sad for the family | The cardigan made him ill, so he should throw it out | Crying late means something is wrong with his brain
exercise: Aisha's best friend's mum died, and Aisha hid from her for a week because she did not know what to say. What helps most? | Being there and saying "I'm sorry, I don't know what to say" | Waiting until her friend stops being sad, then talking | Saying nothing ever, so she does not upset her friend | Telling her friend a funny story so she forgets her mum
exercise: Leo's big brother laughed, "It's a fish, bro, get over it." Is Leo a baby for being sad? | No; how sad you feel depends on how much you cared | Yes; only big animals like dogs are worth being sad about | Yes; boys of nine are too old to be sad about a pet | No, but only if the fish was very expensive to buy
exercise: Ruby's grandad has dementia and no longer remembers her name. What is true? | The illness takes the names, but he often still feels her love | He has chosen to forget her because she visits him far too rarely | He remembers everything and is just pretending to forget | Quizzing him on names every visit will cure the dementia
exercise: What helped Ruby on her visits to her grandad? | Singing his favourite old song and holding his hand | Testing him on everyone's names until he got them right | Staying away until his memory comes back by itself | Showing him he is wrong whenever he forgets something
exercise: Max's last words to his teacher before he died were "this is boring". What is true? | One rude moment does not erase a whole year with the teacher | The teacher surely died believing that Max really hated his lessons | Max can never feel better, because it cannot be fixed now he's gone | It is best to keep this secret from everyone, for good and for ever
exercise: Hannah's grandma is terminally ill, and Hannah lies awake asking, "Will Mum die too?" What is true? | Fearing other deaths is common and does not make them happen | Thinking about death too much can make someone die sooner | She is far too young to have thoughts like that at all | Her mum will certainly die soon too, so the worry is quite right
exercise: Josh shouted "You're killing him!" when the vet put old Smokey to sleep. What does "put to sleep" mean? | The vet helps a very ill animal die peacefully without pain | The vet gives the animal a long nap until it gets better | The vet takes the animal away to live on a quiet farm | The family gives the animal away because they are bored
exercise: Kofi came back to school after his uncle died and everyone acted as if nothing happened. Why do people often stay silent? | They are scared of upsetting him, not because they don't care | They have all decided together that his uncle did not really matter | They are angry with Kofi for having a sad week away | They simply did not notice that Kofi had been away
exercise: What helped Kofi in the end? | A teacher quietly asking, "How are you doing, really?" | Picking fights so that people would notice him | Pretending to everyone that his uncle was still alive and well | Waiting for everybody to forget it ever happened
test: Your mate Kai goes, "if u don't cry at a funeral u didn't love them, simple." What's the best reply? | Not true; some people go numb first and cry weeks later | True; love always shows up as tears on the very day | True, but only for boys, girls always cry straight away | Not true, because nobody ever cries at funerals at all
test: Theo's cousin snaps at him: "you don't even care, you're just stood there." What would have helped Theo more? | Someone saying they didn't cry at their own dad's funeral either | Someone telling him to try harder to cry next time | Someone leaving him alone and never mentioning Nan again | Someone telling him that numb people never really loved
test: Big sister on the phone, Glasgow accent: "Wee Jamie's still greetin' every night, aye, about missin' his grandad on the Sunday." ("Wee" is little; "greetin'" is crying.) What should the family do? | Let him say the Sunday thought out loud and tell him Grandad loved him | Tell him to stop greetin' because big boys move on in a week | Never mention Grandad again in case it makes things worse | Agree that the missed Sunday was a terrible thing he did
test: What would happen if Mia's family stopped saying Rolo's name so they would not upset her? | She would probably feel more alone with her guilt | She would forget Rolo faster and feel better at once | She would stop checking the gate straight away | Nothing at all, because silence always helps grief
test: In a cartoon, the hero's dog dies, he cries once, and the next morning he's totally fine. What's wrong with it? | Real grief usually comes in waves for weeks or months | Nothing; real grief always ends after one good night's sleep | Real people never cry about dogs, only about humans | Real grief only begins a whole year after the loss
test: Leo's brother goes: "lowkey crying over a goldfish is so mid, bro." ("Lowkey" means a bit; "mid" means lame.) Who is right? | Leo; any loss can hurt, and laughing just makes people hide it | The brother; fish are too small to be properly sad about | The brother; being sad about pets is only for really little kids | Neither; it is against the rules to feel sad about pets
test: A girl in class says, "My grandad's got dementia, he doesn't remember me, so there's no point visiting." What could you tell her? | He may still feel her love, like when she sings his old song | She is right, visiting someone with dementia does nothing at all | She should quiz him on her name every visit until he remembers it | Dementia means the person stops having any feelings at all, ever
test: Aisha's mate texts: "yeah nah, I'm just gonna avoid her til she's over her mum, less awkward." What's the problem? | Grieving people often feel lonely when friends go quiet | There is no problem; grief always ends in about a week or so | Avoiding someone is the kindest way to show respect | Her friend will not notice who talks to her anyway
test: Uncle Dai from the Valleys says Josh was "proper tamping" with his dad about Smokey. ("Tamping" means furious.) A week later Josh asks why. What is the honest answer? | Smokey was in pain every day, and the vet helped him die peacefully | The vet wanted the cat because old cats are worth a lot of money | Dad was tired of looking after a cat and wanted a new one | Smokey would have got better on his own if they had waited
test: Hannah keeps thinking: "If I worry hard enough, Grandma won't die tonight." Which is true? | Worry cannot change what happens; asking questions out loud helps more | Worrying very hard every night really can keep someone alive | If she stops worrying, it will be her fault if Grandma dies in the night | Worrying is a magic shield, so she should never stop doing it
test: Max hears another kid say, "Mr Carter was well sound, he made Romans fun." ("Sound" means good, kind.) How might this help Max? | Happy memories from others can soften the stuck "boring" thought | It proves Max was the only one the teacher ever disliked | It means Max must forget Mr Carter as fast as he can | It makes no difference, because memories never change how you feel
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the course of grief following a bereavement? | It commonly recurs in waves which diminish gradually over weeks and months | It invariably concludes within a single day of the bereavement | It is invariably constant in intensity for the remainder of one's life | It is experienced exclusively by adults and never by children
exam: Identify the principal reason for which guilt is frequently experienced subsequent to a death. | The mind seeks a cause it might have altered, which feels less helpless | The bereaved person has invariably caused the death in question | Guilt is a legal penalty imposed by the courts upon all surviving relatives | Guilt arises only where the deceased expressed anger beforehand
exam: It is given that a child exhibited no tears at a funeral. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Initial numbness is common and does not indicate an absence of love | The absence of tears constitutes proof of an absence of affection | The child shall be incapable of grief for the remainder of his life | Tears are expressly prohibited at funerals in most jurisdictions
exam: In respect of the procedure whereby a veterinarian is said to "put an animal to sleep", determine which description is correct. | A very ill animal is assisted to die peacefully and without pain | An animal is induced into prolonged slumber pending its recovery | An animal is relocated to a rural property for its retirement | An animal is surrendered to new owners at the family's request
